IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cacl-passenger`

-- Civil Aviation (Carriers' Liability) Act 1959 -- worked cases, from the seat of the air passenger.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (Compilation No. 30, C2021C00236), never copied from the encoding's output. All facts are
-- HYPOTHETICAL; no scenario raised this Act. Whether a country is a party to a Convention is an input: the cases say which
-- answer they assume.
--
-- The six regime inputs, in order: where the journey begins and ends; both ends parties to the 1999 Montreal Convention;
-- to the Hague Protocol; to the Warsaw Convention; carried for reward (or free by an airline); a commercial flight.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Which law governs the flight

-- Perth to Singapore and back, both taken to be States Parties to all three: the 1999 Montreal Convention prevails (Art 55).
#ASSERT `Which law governs my flight` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `the 1999 Montreal Convention (Part IA)`
-- A return trip from Perth with an agreed stop in Bali is international carriage (Art 1(2)): Australia need only be a party.
#ASSERT `Which law governs my flight` `both in Australia, with an agreed stopping place in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `the 1999 Montreal Convention (Part IA)`
-- Hypothetical: the other country is a party to the Hague Protocol but not to Montreal: Part II.
#ASSERT `Which law governs my flight` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague (Part II)`
-- Hypothetical: a party to the 1929 Warsaw Convention only: Part III.
#ASSERT `Which law governs my flight` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `the Warsaw Convention without the Hague Protocol (Part III)`
-- Hypothetical: a party to none of them, on a commercial flight: Part IV, s 27(1)(d).
#ASSERT `Which law governs my flight` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `Part IV, as international carriage no Convention covers`
-- Perth to Sydney on a scheduled airline: Part IV domestic carriage (s 27(1)(a)).
#ASSERT `Which law governs my flight` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `Part IV, as domestic carriage`
-- Perth to Darwin: s 27(1)(b).
#ASSERT `Which law governs my flight` `in Australia, with one or both ends in a Territory` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `Part IV, as domestic carriage`
-- Perth to Broome, no landing outside WA: not this Act (s 27).
#ASSERT `Which law governs my flight` `in one State, with no landing outside that State` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `a flight that begins and ends in one State, with no landing outside it, is not carriage this Act governs (s 27): State law applies`
-- Perth to Broome with a landing in the Northern Territory: deemed interstate carriage (s 27(3)).
#ASSERT `Which law governs my flight` `in one State, with a landing in another State or a Territory` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `Part IV, as domestic carriage`
-- A friend's private plane from Perth to Adelaide, nothing paid: neither a Convention nor Part IV.
#ASSERT `Which law governs my flight` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `this flight is neither carriage for reward (or free carriage by an air transport undertaking) under a Convention nor commercial carriage under Part IV (s 27)`
-- London to Paris, hypothetically outside every Convention: Part IV does not reach it.
#ASSERT `Which law governs my flight` `both outside Australia` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `no Convention applies to this journey, and Part IV covers only carriage within Australia or between Australia and another country (s 27)`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Death or bodily injury

#ASSERT `Is the carrier liable for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the carrier is liable whatever the cause, and cannot exclude or limit its liability up to 100 000 SDRs; above that it escapes only if it proves the damage was not due to its negligence or was solely due to a third party (Montreal Arts 17(1), 21)`
-- Injured in the terminal car park, not embarking: no liability under the Convention.
#ASSERT `Is the carrier liable for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: the carrier is liable only for an accident that took place on board the aircraft or in the course of embarking or disembarking`
#ASSERT `Is the carrier liable for my injury` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the carrier is liable for damage from an accident on board or in embarking or disembarking (s 28)`
#ASSERT `Is the carrier liable for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes, unless the carrier proves that it and its servants and agents took all necessary measures to avoid the damage or that it was impossible to take them (Warsaw Arts 17, 20)`
#ASSERT `Is the carrier liable for my injury` `in one State, with no landing outside that State` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `a flight that begins and ends in one State, with no landing outside it, is not carriage this Act governs (s 27): State law applies`

-- The limit. Inputs after the six: the airline proves no negligence or sole third-party fault; intent or recklessness;
-- an Australian international carrier; the higher limit the ticket states (0 if none).
-- Montreal, the airline cannot prove it was not negligent: no limit (Art 21(2)).
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 0 EQUALS `answered` `no limit`
-- Montreal, the airline proves a third party alone was to blame: 100 000 SDRs (Art 21(2)(b)).
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in SDRs` 100000)
-- ... and the ticket stipulates 150 000 SDRs (Art 25).
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE 150000 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in SDRs` 150000)
-- Perth to Sydney: $925 000 (s 31(1)(a), (1AA)).
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in Australian dollars` 925000)
-- ... where the ticket states $1 000 000 (s 31(1)(c)).
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 1000000 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in Australian dollars` 1000000)
-- ... and wilful misconduct does not lift a Part IV limit.
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in Australian dollars` 925000)
-- Part IV international carriage: 480 000 SDRs (s 31(1A), (1B)).
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in SDRs` 480000)
-- Part II, an Australian international carrier: 260 000 SDRs (s 11A(1)(a)); a foreign carrier: 250 000 francs (Art 22(1)).
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in SDRs` 260000)
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in gold francs` 250000)
-- ... recklessness with knowledge that damage would probably result: no limit (Hague Art 25).
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` `no limit`
-- Part III, a foreign carrier: 125 000 francs (Warsaw Art 22(1)); an Australian one: 260 000 SDRs (s 21A).
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in gold francs` 125000)
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my injury` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in SDRs` 260000)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Baggage. Inputs after the six: which baggage; during the carriage by air; an inherent defect; the airline proves all
-- necessary measures; the airline's fault (for baggage kept).

#ASSERT `Is the airline liable for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the carrier is liable for checked baggage destroyed, lost or damaged on board or while in its charge, whatever the cause (Montreal Art 17(2))`
-- Montreal is strict for checked baggage: proving all necessary measures does not help the airline.
#ASSERT `Is the airline liable for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the carrier is liable for checked baggage destroyed, lost or damaged on board or while in its charge, whatever the cause (Montreal Art 17(2))`
#ASSERT `Is the airline liable for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no, to the extent the damage resulted from the inherent defect, quality or vice of the baggage (Montreal Art 17(2))`
#ASSERT `Is the airline liable for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: the occurrence did not take place during the carriage by air (Montreal Art 17(2); Warsaw Art 18; s 29(2)-(3))`
#ASSERT `Is the airline liable for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `baggage I kept with me` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: for baggage you kept with you, the carrier is liable only if the damage resulted from its fault or that of its servants or agents (Montreal Art 17(2))`
#ASSERT `Is the airline liable for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `baggage I kept with me` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: for baggage you kept with you, the damage resulted from the carrier's fault or that of its servants or agents (Montreal Art 17(2))`
-- Perth to Sydney: liable unless the airline proves all necessary measures (s 29(1)).
#ASSERT `Is the airline liable for my baggage` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes, because the occurrence took place during the carriage by air and the carrier has not proved that it took all necessary measures (Warsaw Arts 18, 20; s 29(1))`
#ASSERT `Is the airline liable for my baggage` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: the carrier proves that it and its servants and agents took all necessary measures, or that it was impossible to take them (Warsaw Art 20; s 29(1))`
-- s 29 covers baggage kept with the passenger too.
#ASSERT `Is the airline liable for my baggage` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `baggage I kept with me` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes, because the occurrence took place during the carriage by air and the carrier has not proved that it took all necessary measures (Warsaw Arts 18, 20; s 29(1))`
#ASSERT `Is the airline liable for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `baggage I kept with me` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `the Warsaw Conventions' liability for objects the passenger takes charge of (unchecked baggage) is not encoded`

-- The baggage limit. Inputs after the six: includes checked baggage; its weight in kg; a special declaration; intent or
-- recklessness; the higher limit the ticket states.
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE 23 FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in SDRs` 1000)
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE 23 TRUE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` `the sum I declared when I checked the baggage in`
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE 23 FALSE TRUE 0 EQUALS `answered` `no limit`
-- Perth to Sydney: $3 000 with checked baggage (s 31(2)); $300 for baggage kept only (s 31(3)); $5 000 if the ticket says so.
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE 23 FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in Australian dollars` 3000)
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in Australian dollars` 300)
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE 23 FALSE FALSE 5000 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in Australian dollars` 5000)
-- Part II, a 23 kg bag: 250 francs a kilogram, 5 750 francs (Art 22(2)(a)); baggage kept only: 5 000 francs (Art 22(3)).
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE 23 FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in gold francs` 5750)
#ASSERT `The most the airline can be made to pay for my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS `answered` (`a limit in gold francs` 5000)

-- A missing checked bag that ought to have arrived on 1 March 2026: the 21 days after 1 March end with 22 March, so from
-- 23 March 2026 (Art 17(3)); sooner if the airline admits the loss.
#ASSERT `From when may I treat my checked bag as lost` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE (Date 1 3 2026) FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) EQUALS `answered` (Date 23 3 2026)
#ASSERT `From when may I treat my checked bag as lost` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE (Date 1 3 2026) TRUE (Date 5 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` (Date 5 3 2026)
#ASSERT `From when may I treat my checked bag as lost` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE (Date 1 3 2026) FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) EQUALS `not covered` `only the 1999 Montreal Convention (Art 17(3)) lets a passenger treat checked baggage as lost after 21 days`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Delay. Inputs after the six: the airline proves the measures required; intent or recklessness.
#ASSERT `Can I claim for the delay to my flight` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`a claim for the damage the delay caused, up to a limit` (`a limit in SDRs` 4150))
#ASSERT `Can I claim for the delay to my flight` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no claim: the carrier proves that it and its servants and agents took all the measures that could reasonably be required (Montreal) or all necessary measures (Warsaw) to avoid the damage, or that it was impossible to take them`
#ASSERT `Can I claim for the delay to my flight` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` (`a claim for the damage the delay caused, up to a limit` `no limit`)
#ASSERT `Can I claim for the delay to my flight` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`a claim for the damage the delay caused, up to a limit` (`a limit in gold francs` 250000))
-- A late Perth to Sydney flight: Part IV gives no claim for delay at all.
#ASSERT `Can I claim for the delay to my flight` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `Part IV imposes no liability for delay: a claim for a late domestic flight lies, if at all, under the contract or other law, not this Act`
#ASSERT `Can I claim for the delay to my flight` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `Part IV imposes no liability for delay: a claim for a late domestic flight lies, if at all, under the contract or other law, not this Act`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Complaints. Inputs after the six: which baggage; what happened; the day received; the day placed (or due to be placed)
-- at my disposal; the day my journey ended.
-- Montreal, a checked bag received damaged on 10 March 2026: by 17 March (Art 31(2), seven days).
#ASSERT `By when must I complain about my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` `it was damaged` (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 9 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` (`complain to the carrier in writing by` (Date 17 3 2026))
-- Montreal, a delayed bag given to me on 12 March 2026: by 2 April 2026 (twenty-one days).
#ASSERT `By when must I complain about my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` `it was delayed` (Date 12 3 2026) (Date 12 3 2026) (Date 9 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` (`complain to the carrier in writing by` (Date 2 4 2026))
-- Montreal, a whole bag lost: Art 31 sets no time (damage and delay only).
#ASSERT `By when must I complain about my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` `a whole item was lost or destroyed` (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 9 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` `the Act sets no time for a complaint about this`
#ASSERT `By when must I complain about my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `baggage I kept with me` `it was damaged` (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 9 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` `the Act sets no time for a complaint about this`
-- Part III (Warsaw unamended): three days for damage, fourteen for delay (Art 26(2)).
#ASSERT `By when must I complain about my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` `it was damaged` (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 9 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` (`complain to the carrier in writing by` (Date 13 3 2026))
#ASSERT `By when must I complain about my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` `it was delayed` (Date 12 3 2026) (Date 12 3 2026) (Date 9 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` (`complain to the carrier in writing by` (Date 26 3 2026))
-- Part II (Hague): seven days for damage.
#ASSERT `By when must I complain about my baggage` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` `it was damaged` (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 9 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` (`complain to the carrier in writing by` (Date 17 3 2026))
-- Perth to Sydney: damage to a checked bag, three days after receipt (s 30(2)(a)).
#ASSERT `By when must I complain about my baggage` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` `it was damaged` (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 9 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` (`complain to the carrier in writing by` (Date 13 3 2026))
-- ... a whole checked bag lost, due on 10 March: twenty-one days, by 31 March 2026 (s 30(2)(b)).
#ASSERT `By when must I complain about my baggage` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` `a whole item was lost or destroyed` (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 9 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` (`complain to the carrier in writing by` (Date 31 3 2026))
-- ... cabin baggage damaged, the journey ending on 9 March: three days from then, 12 March 2026 (s 30(2)(c)).
#ASSERT `By when must I complain about my baggage` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `baggage I kept with me` `it was damaged` (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 9 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` (`complain to the carrier in writing by` (Date 12 3 2026))
#ASSERT `By when must I complain about my baggage` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE `checked baggage` `it was delayed` (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 10 3 2026) (Date 9 3 2026) EQUALS `answered` `the Act sets no time for a complaint about this`

-- Suing. Inputs after the six: arrived; the day it arrived; the day it ought to have arrived; the day the carriage stopped.
#ASSERT `The last day to sue the airline` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE (Date 15 1 2026) (Date 15 1 2026) (Date 15 1 2026) EQUALS `answered` (Date 15 1 2028)
-- The aircraft never arrives: due 15 January, the carriage stopped 20 January 2026. Part IV takes the later (s 34) ...
#ASSERT `The last day to sue the airline` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) (Date 15 1 2026) (Date 20 1 2026) EQUALS `answered` (Date 20 1 2028)
-- ... and the encoding takes the earlier under the Convention (finding 7).
#ASSERT `The last day to sue the airline` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE (Date 1 1 2026) (Date 15 1 2026) (Date 20 1 2026) EQUALS `answered` (Date 15 1 2028)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Damages
#ASSERT `Is a ticket term lowering the airline's liability valid` `one end in Australia and the other in another country` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` FALSE
#ASSERT `Is a ticket term lowering the airline's liability valid` `in two different Australian States` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `answered` FALSE
#ASSERT `Is a ticket term lowering the airline's liability valid` `in one State, with no landing outside that State` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `a flight that begins and ends in one State, with no landing outside it, is not carriage this Act governs (s 27): State law applies`
#ASSERT `Can my damages be reduced because of this payment` `an insurance payout on my death or injury` EQUALS `answered` FALSE
#ASSERT `Can my damages be reduced because of this payment` `a pension, social service benefit or repatriation benefit paid because of the death or injury` EQUALS `answered` FALSE
#ASSERT `Can my damages be reduced because of this payment` `some other payment` EQUALS `not covered` `the Act lists the payments not to be deducted (ss 9G, 15, 38); whether another payment reduces damages is for the general law`
-- $500 000 assessed, reduced by 20%: $400 000, no limit.
#ASSERT `What I recover after contributory negligence` 500000 20 0 EQUALS 400000
-- $2 000 000 reduced by 25% is $1 500 000, under a $1 650 000 limit.
#ASSERT `What I recover after contributory negligence` 2000000 25 1650000 EQUALS 1500000
-- $2 000 000 reduced by 10% is $1 800 000, cut to the $1 650 000 limit (s 9H(4)).
#ASSERT `What I recover after contributory negligence` 2000000 10 1650000 EQUALS 1650000
-- 100 000 SDRs at a hypothetical $2.05 a SDR on judgment day.
#ASSERT `The SDR limit in Australian dollars` 100000 2.05 EQUALS 205000

-- A passenger's death
#ASSERT `Is the death action for my benefit` `spouse or de facto partner` FALSE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT `Is the death action for my benefit` `foster-sibling, foster-child or guardian` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT (NOT (`Is the death action for my benefit` `foster-sibling, foster-child or guardian` FALSE TRUE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Is the death action for my benefit` `none of these` TRUE TRUE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Is the death action for my benefit` `sibling, step-brother, step-sister, half-brother or half-sister` FALSE FALSE TRUE))
#ASSERT (NOT (`Is the death action for my benefit` `parent, step-parent or grandparent` FALSE TRUE FALSE))
